---
import DocumentLayout from '../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';
import { pages } from '../data/pages';
---

<DocumentLayout
  title="Small tools. A bigger picture."
  description="PixelTools helps images fit into your life, with fewer steps and clearer choices."
  eyebrow="ABOUT PIXELTOOLS"
  metaTitle={pages.about.metaTitle}
  metaDescription={pages.about.metaDescription}
  pageType="AboutPage"
>
  <p>
    An application form needs a smaller photo. A website needs lighter images. A
    profile picture needs a different shape. These are ordinary tasks, and they
    should feel ordinary to finish.
  </p>
  <p>
    PixelTools brings ten focused image utilities into a simple browser
    workspace. Pick an image, choose the settings, inspect the real result, and
    download it. No account, conversion service, or subscription is required.
  </p>
  <div class="about-values">
    <div>
      <h2>Private by design</h2>
      <p>
        Core processing happens on your device. Selected image bytes, filenames,
        and metadata are not sent to a server.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>Clear about the result</h2>
      <p>
        A target is a goal. A format is a real encoding. A size comparison uses
        the actual generated file.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>Room to get it right</h2>
      <p>
        Keep the original, compare the preview, and read the limitations before
        sharing the next version.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>A little context</h2>
      <p>
        Each tool explains its controls, tradeoffs, examples, and supported
        formats. Useful tools should help you understand the output.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <h2>Built for everyday images</h2>
  <p>
    The toolkit is for students, creators, professionals, designers, and anyone
    who needs a quick image adjustment. It supports static JPG, PNG, and WebP
    within clearly stated device-friendly limits.
  </p>
  <p>
    Browser-based processing is not perfect security. Use a device and browser
    you trust, manage your downloaded files, and review metadata and visible
    image content before publishing.
  </p>
  <a class="btn primary" href="/tools">
    Find your image tool →
  </a>
</DocumentLayout>
